# Lowest Common Ancestor of a Binary Search Tree — LeetCode 235

## Pattern
Binary Search Tree (BST) + DFS + Recursion

## Approach
1. If `root == null`, return `null`.
2. Ensure `p.val <= q.val` by swapping the nodes if necessary.
3. If `root.val < p.val`, both nodes are on the right side. Search right.
4. If `root.val > q.val`, both nodes are on the left side. Search left.
5. Otherwise, the current root is the LCA because the nodes lie on different sides, or one of them is the current node.

## Corrected Java Code

```java
class Solution {
    public TreeNode lowestCommonAncestor(
            TreeNode root, TreeNode p, TreeNode q) {

        if (root == null) return null;

        if (p.val > q.val) {
            TreeNode temp = p;
            p = q;
            q = temp;
        }

        if (root.val < p.val) {
            return lowestCommonAncestor(root.right, p, q);
        }

        if (root.val > q.val) {
            return lowestCommonAncestor(root.left, p, q);
        }

        return root;
    }
}
```

## Dry Run

BST:

```text
        6
       / \
      2   8
     / \ / \
    0  4 7  9
      / \
     3   5
```

Given: `p = 2`, `q = 4`

1. `p.val < q.val`, so no swap.
2. At node `6`, `6 > 4`, so move left.
3. At node `2`, the current value lies between `p.val` and `q.val`, including equality.
4. Return node `2`.

Output: `2`

## Complexity

- **Time:** `O(h)` — where `h` is the BST height.
  - Balanced BST: `O(log n)`
  - Worst case: `O(n)`
- **Space:** `O(h)` — recursion stack.

## Key Takeaways

- Sorting `p` and `q` by value is optional; it just simplifies comparisons.
- Your original code's main issue is that it doesn't return the recursive result directly and relies on an instance variable `ans`.
- The final `else` case can simply return `root`; separate strict-between checks are unnecessary because equality is already handled.
- The solution works when one target node is the ancestor of the other.
